One necessary edge domain for a three-colour square
==================================================

Proposed by /root; checked and written by /root/j3_recovery,
17 October 2026. This is a conditional finite cover, not an exclusion
or a historical novelty claim. The setting is the acquired order54
problem: a finite simple connected quartic graph G, girth six, exactly
one hexagon through every edge. Fix a hexagon C and suppose its actual
hexagon layers have t=4 and R=C4 with three opposite-anchor colours.

Normalization without selecting exterior attachments
----------------------------------------------------

Proper edge-colouring of a four-cycle with three colours uses one
colour twice on opposite edges and the other colours once each. The
dihedral symmetries of C induce every permutation of its three
opposite pairs. Choose these pairs as A={0,3}, B={1,4}, D={2,5},
and label the subdivided square so t0,t2 have colour A, t1 has B,
and t3 has D. Write ti between zi and z(i+1), modulo four. Reflecting
or rotating this square and naming C does not constrain any remaining
edge of G.

Every anchor ci has two actual L1 children, called x(i,0),x(i,1).
Opposite T vertices have disjoint L1 parents by the accepted square
attachment lemma. Parents belonging to different colours have
different anchors. All eight T parents are consequently distinct.
Independently at each anchor, name the parents as follows:

* t0 has x(0,0),x(3,0); t2 has x(0,1),x(3,1).
* t1 has x(1,0),x(4,0).
* t3 has x(2,0),x(5,0).

Each of these eight parents has two S children. Each of the four
unused parents x(1,1),x(2,1),x(4,1),x(5,1) has three S children.
Name them s(i,e,j) arbitrarily within each actual parent. These are
28 different vertices because an S vertex has one L1 parent. There
is no naming according to Z attachments and no matching is imposed.

Let K contain just C's six edges, all twelve C--L1 edges, the eight
displayed L1--T edges, all eight ti--zi/ti--z(i+1) edges, and all
28 displayed L1--S edges. Its order is54 and size is62. Every vertex
outside S union Z has degree four; each z has fixed degree two and
each s has fixed degree one. The acquired t4 layer facts give no
Z--Z edges. Therefore every unspecified edge lies either in Z--S
or in S--S.

Necessary exclusions and exact domain size
------------------------------------------

The repeated-colour lemma forbids every Z--S edge whose S anchor
is0 or3. Its proof is not restricted to two-colour R: for any z,
one of the two A-coloured T vertices is adjacent to z. Attaching
through its parent gives a four-cycle; attaching through the other
child at that anchor gives two distinct hexagons sharing the Z--S
edge. The two A parents exhaust the children at each of0 and3.
This is the general repeated-colour claim already proved in
hexagon_ball_square_repeated_colours.rst.

For any incident pair z--t, also forbid attaching z to an S child
of either L1 parent of t. Such an attachment creates the four-cycle
z--t--x--s--z. Impose no other restrictions on Z--S pairs in the
candidate domain. Outside anchors0,3 there are20 S vertices. Each z
is incident with exactly one of t1,t3. Its two parents have two S
children each, so exactly four of those20 are removed. The other
incident T has colour A and adds no further removal. Thus there
are16 allowed S neighbours for each z, or64 distinct Z--S pairs.

Permit all S--S pairs at cyclic anchor distance at least two. The
necessity of this restriction is the acquired anchor argument:
equal parents give a triangle, distinct parents at the same anchor
give a five-cycle, and adjacent anchors give a hexagon sharing a C
edge. The S multiplicities at anchors0 through5 are(4,5,5,4,5,5).
Hence the number of permitted S--S pairs is exactly

  binom(28,2) - (2*binom(4,2)+4*binom(5,2))
               - (20+25+20+20+25+20) = 378-52-130 =196.

The combined domain contains260 optional pairs, not266. It includes
all64 allowed Z--S and all196 allowed S--S pairs, without any further
identification, assignment, symmetry breaking or choice of attachment.

Coverage and conditional converse
--------------------------------

The preceding naming gives an isomorphism of every original graph
in this three-colour subcase to K plus a subset of this exact domain.
Every z needs two extra neighbours, so a quartic completion selects
exactly eight Z--S edges. The28 S vertices need84 new incidences;
eight are used by those edges. Their remaining76 incidences give
exactly38 S--S edges. Total size becomes62+8+38=108, as required.
The degree conditions do not assume distinct S endpoints among
Z--S edges; such an impossible configuration may remain in this
necessary weakening and will have to be excluded by cycle conditions.

Conversely, if a chosen subset of this domain produces a quartic
graph with the full original cycle properties, it satisfies the
stated conditional hypotheses. K is connected and remains so after
adding edges. Its order is54 and its displayed C is a hexagon.
Only x vertices have edges to C outside C, and only S,T have edges
to L1 outside C. The S vertices keep exactly one L1 parent, T keep
their two parents and two Z neighbours, and Z have distance exactly
three from C. Thus the actual layer sets and contracted coloured
square are precisely the displayed ones. No sufficiency is claimed
for degree and allowed-edge restrictions alone.

Literal encoder and certificate boundary
---------------------------------------

hexagon_ball_three_colour_model.py supplies this literal K and full
optional domain. It reuses the prior fixed-graph cycle inspection
and pin functions, checks the layer sizes/degrees/anchor multiplicities,
both optional pair counts and the8/38 residual counts, and writes the
same model grammar. Its small fixed-K cycle observation is only an
inspection of forced edges, not a completion enumeration.

The unchanged graph_completion_final_proof/search.py and
graph_completion_certificate/cnf.py can apply exactly the existing
degree-four necessary clauses and justified shared-hexagon cuts to
this model. Every original graph satisfies all those clauses. Only
a separately rebuilt, byte-identical CNF and independently checked
final RUP refutation can exclude this conditional subcase. A relaxed
model, timeout or unverified UNSAT claim does not do so. The exact
original-edge hexagon existence property remains unchecked by the
necessary weakening; no SAT witness is asserted from it.

This proof does not itself run or release a solver, retire an old
forced domain, exclude another t value, or establish global order54
nonexistence. The separate two-colour files remain unchanged.

